WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Today, I had a couple of
meetings near Manchester,

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.520
and I had a bit of spare time,
so I visited three river crossings,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.920
all a bit strange, and all
with something in common.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.920
Although, I guess technically
these are canal crossings.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.520
But some of the canal is a diverted river.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.480
So... I went with river crossings,
it sounds better.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.360
My first stop was the Hulmes Ferry,
and I took my GoPro and my drone with me.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.520
Like all the three crossings, the Hulmes Ferry
goes over the Manchester Ship Canal.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.840
That canal runs for nearly 60 kilometres,

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.400
starting in the Mersey Estuary
in the Irish Sea near Liverpool,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.920
and running through to Manchester.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.600
It's more than a century old.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.800
The canal is a massive engineering achievement.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.000
But it's not like the builders just smashed
through anything that was in their way.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.840
It was the Industrial Revolution, but still.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.040
The Act of Parliament that gave
permission for the canal, back in 1885,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.880
included a lot of requirements to make sure
all the landowners along the route were...

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.440
well, if not happy, at least satisfied.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.840
Embankments or channels had to be
maintained or built,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.240
access had to be provided,
fees had to be paid.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.040
300 pages of
technical legal details,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.800
some of which still apply to this day.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.240
Also, you'd think that by now,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.560
every bit of UK law would be
online and freely available,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.440
but no, I found most of this

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.280
in archived Freedom of Information
on obscure things from years ago.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.240
Anyway. One of those legal requirements
from 1885 is a ferry,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.040
"by night and by day whenever required",

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.040
to replace a low bridge
they had to knock down.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.440
If I'm being honest, there's not really
much need to have a ferry like that

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.040
in the 21st century. You wouldn't start
a new ferry service there today.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.400
Yes, it was useful in the past,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.920
it's an hour's walk to get
to the other side...

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.800
but unless you happen to live
really close to one side

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.560
and work really close to the other,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.920
it's not much use.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.040
For almost every practical purpose,
it's quicker to drive.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.280
There are regular passengers:
so the ferry driver said

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.000
in an interview a couple
of years ago, but not many.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.640
It's mostly walkers and cyclists
out for the fun of it.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.400
But my travel on it was logged,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.080
one more on the count of "people
who actually use this ferry".

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.960
And I'll get back to why that happened later.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.880
A few kilometres west was my second stop,
the Thelwall Ferry.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.040
Actually, it might be "Tell-wall".
I didn’t meet anyone to ask,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.640
and there’s no official web site
or information about it,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.240
but it’s much the same arrangement,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.840
although this time with a small charge.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.760
Eleven pence.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.360
Oh, and that boat doesn't have an engine,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.120
it's just powered by a hard-working man
rowing you across, just as there's been

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.720
for more than 120 years.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.240
Not the same man, obviously,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.760
and not the same boat either.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.000
And when I turned up, there was a small sign
in the window of the ferryman's cottage,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.440
saying that the service was suspended
due to low water levels in the canal.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.520
I'll be honest, those water levels looked
fine to me, but I'm not the expert.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.240
Low water levels aside,
the ferry continues,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.120
because the law says that “the Company shall work
the said ferry whenever required”.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.040
The Company, by the way, is called
the Peel Group these days,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.920
after a lot of buying and selling
and corporate renaming and mergers.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.800
They're an enormous property investment firm
who own the entire Manchester Ship Canal

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.400
along with what seems like every major bit of land
and private infrastructure around Manchester.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.680
I've reached out to them with interview
requests before for other stuff,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.680
and they've always turned me down,

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.280
so for this video I have to rely
on secondary sources,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.080
newspaper articles and quotes.
I don't like doing that,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.760
but from the look of some of those articles,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.240
it's not just me that they've
turn down requests from.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.480
Which is fair, they're absolutely
within their rights to do that.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.600
And, if I'm honest, the second reason that
I'm sorta-improvising here and relying

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.880
on those quotes and articles...

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.920
I only found out I was travelling
to Manchester yesterday morning!

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.840
So I had to spend the entire rest
of the day just researching at home

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.280
trying to get the law right.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.240
So: the Thelwall Ferry. Or Tell-wall.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.160
About 600 crossings a year, they say:

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.760
some days there's barely anyone, other days
a whole walking club might turn up

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.240
and want to go across
at eleven pence each.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.440
The third crossing, inbetween those two
ferries, is the Warburton Toll Bridge.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.640
Which is a deeply frustrating bridge
for a lot of people,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.480
because the toll to cross in a car

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:47.600
is twelve pence.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.680
Or 25 pence, if you want a day pass.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.720
And there is often a queue.
Cash only, no change given.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.760
And it seems almost farcical
to have a toll collector,

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.000
and a barrier, and sometimes
very long queues...

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.840
for twelve pence a time.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.320
Except: when I got there
and I drove up…

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.320
No charges! But, wanting to get the shot,
I turned round and went back over,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.920
and they were charging that way,

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.520
but no day passes either. Just a man
with a coin bucket and no change.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.360
Uh... no daily ticket?

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:14.480
That's 20p then! Thank you.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.160
[Toll operator] "Right, cheers pal."

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.320
One of the nice things about
improvising this video is

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.560
that I’ve seen things
as they actually were,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.960
not as the PR teams
would want them to be.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.920
Anyway, this toll. It made sense
when the bridge was built.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.480
"For every Carriage drawn or propelled by
Steam or any Means other than Animal Power,

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:31.920
"Two Shillings and Sixpence".

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.160
Back then, that was worth collecting,
adjusted for inflation,

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.280
that'd be about £15.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.640
But the law did not mention inflation.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.120
The company could choose to
waive the tolls at any time,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.800
they did that during the
Covid lockdown last year,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.800
and I guess they're doing that
one way now.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.280
But if you stop collecting tolls
for the long term,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.600
it's a lot more difficult
to bring them back.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.600
And they'd still be required
to maintain the bridge.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.560
If there's one car in each direction
every 30 seconds on average,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.080
and looking at that, yeah, there is:

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.400
well, that's still about a quarter
million pounds of revenue every year.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.640
So you'll see people talk about
story of old quirky British laws

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.040
that mean you still have these ferries,
and you still that toll bridge,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.640
oh, and they must be maintained by law,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.040
and isn't it weird what happens
in rural Britain?

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.440
But I don't think the law is the
reason those crossings are still there.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.640
Because there are two ways you could
interpret "whenever required"

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.240
in those old legal documents.
"Whenever required" could mean:

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.560
whenever anyone asks for it.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.040
If someone turns up, any hour of any day,
the ferry must be there waiting for them.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.200
It's a valid interpretation, but that's not
how those words been read through history.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.800
And it's not how they're being read now.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.480
The Hulmes ferry only runs for a few hours,
three or four days a week,

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.680
and never in winter.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.000
And it stopped running for
a year back in 2010,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.040
it took a campaign and local effort
to get it reinstated.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.600
As for the Thelwall Ferry,
it runs most days,

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:50.800
but only for a few hours.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.000
And apparently not when the water’s low?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.880
Remember I said my crossing was
logged and counted?

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.560
Well, you can also interpret
"whenever required" to mean

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.800
"if there is reasonable demand".

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.160
There's an unofficial web site
for the Hulmes ferry, and it says:

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.680
"use it or lose it".

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.640
Those old laws mention other ferries.
They've all gone.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.680
I don't know if they were removed
through legal process or just

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.640
forgotten about, because ultimately:
no-one's complaining.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:20.160
And if someone did complain, who in
government is going to take action

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.640
about a ferry that
almost no-one uses?

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.760
I think these ferries,
and the 12 pence toll,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.480
have stuck around because
it would be more effort

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.520
for the giant corporation
to try and stop them.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.680
It's a minor thing for a
multi-billion-pound company

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.520
to pay for a couple of ferry-boats,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.400
compared to having to deal with
angry locals and bad press.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.760
And I think the toll on that bridge
has stayed at 12 pence for so long

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.200
because increasing it would have
required a change in the law,

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.800
and trying to get that is way more
time-consuming and expensive

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.160
than just keeping things as they are.
In our own lives,

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.760
we all make do with a lot of small
bothersome things and irritations

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.760
because it's less work than
trying to fix them.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.000
Different scale, same principle.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.720
Although I'm not saying the ferries
are a bothersome thing,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:03.920
I think they're brilliant,

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.800
I think we should have more
quirky useful things funded

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.880
by multi-billion-dollar corporations.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.280
I'm just saying that the company has
many bigger things to deal with.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.320
If they wanted to, they could probably
get away without the ferries.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.720
And at a guess… I reckon it's not
worth the hassle of not running them.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.120
But one thing is changing soon.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.640
That bridge needs expensive refurbishment,
maybe even replacement soon.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:27.680
Which means that the company
has noticed it, and suddenly

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.080
changing the law might be
worth their time.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.400
There was a consultation process
for the locals last month.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.560
The company would like to raise
the toll up to a whole...

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:36.400
...one pound.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.720
And replace the ticket booths
with automatic systems.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.320
So if you do want the novelty
of the 12 pence toll...

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.080
now's the time to go.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.240
Because the law will probably
change quite soon.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.600
As for the ferries... I do hope that
the locals will make sure that

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:57.840
removing them never becomes
the easy option.

